2026-09-27  Jordan Ninin  <jordan.ninin@ensta.fr>

	GAOL v5, version 5.0.0, continues GAOL 4.2.2. doc/differences.md
	gives each change and why; the main ones:

	* 3rd/math-core: CORE-MATH, compiled into libgaol, bounds every
	elementary function, correctly rounded; mathlib is gone.  The
	functions IEEE 1788-2015 requires and recommends are added (exp2,
	exp10, log2, log10, cbrt, expm1, sinpi..., fma, the integer
	functions), with the names of the standard in gaol_ieee1788.
	* gaol/: the tightest bounds of the arithmetic, the reverse
	functions accurate in the sense of IEEE 1788-2015, the empty set
	[NaN, NaN], numbers read exactly whatever the C library, the
	possibly relations and == and != removed, one grammar reading every
	string, a reentrant lexer and a pure parser, the rounding direction
	checked once per operation, the *_dn() and *_up() of doubles no
	longer public, the intervals of floats left to the developers of
	GAOL (GAOL_FLOAT_INTERVALS).
	* CMakeLists.txt: a CMake build, the one to use, which finds GAOL
	with find_package(gaol) and makes the archive of the sources and
	the packages (CPack); make dist is gone.
	* tests/: tests of the bounds against the exact results, and the
	continuous integration on Linux, macOS and Windows
	(.github/workflows).
	* manual/v5: the manual of GAOL v5; manual/v4 keeps the manual of
	GAOL 4.

	The three builds made alike:

	* configure.ac: the version is 5.0.0, not "5.0.0" with its quotes;
	AC_INIT gives jordan.ninin@ensta.fr for the bug reports and
	https://github.com/Jordan08/GAOL.  --enable-debug builds as the
	Debug build of CMake (-g, no optimization nor NDEBUG,
	GAOL_DEBUGGING), where it gave g++ alone -g -ansi -Weffc++ -pedantic
	on top of --enable-optimize.  --with-tests and --with-examples.  The
	checks nothing read are gone (AC_FUNC_MALLOC, AC_FUNC_REALLOC,
	AC_HEADER_STDC, AC_C_BIGENDIAN, AC_CHECK_SIZEOF...), and so are
	CppUnit, the dl library, doxygen, dot, AC_PROG_YACC, --enable-relations,
	AC_REVISION, the release of the RPM and the line "relations =" of the
	summary.  no-define: no PACKAGE nor VERSION.
	* gaol/gaol_configuration.h.in, gaol/gaol_configuration_meson.h.in,
	cmake/gaol_configuration.h.in: the same macros with the three builds
	(doc/building.md lists them, and says which the sources do not read);
	the template of configure is written by hand.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h, gaol/gaol_version.h: include the generated
	configuration with every compiler.  gaol_config_msvc.h,
	gaol_config_mingw.h, gaol_version_msvc.h, gaol_version_mingw.h:
	removed.  PACKAGE and VERSION are no longer undefined.
	* gaol/: the sources no longer read HAVE_LIMITS, HAVE_CASSERT,
	HAVE_CLOCK, GETRUSAGE_IN_HEADER, HAVE_NEXTAFTER, HAVE_ISNAN nor
	SIZEOF_LONG_INT (gaol_limits.h, gaol_assert.h and gaol_profile.cpp,
	which read some of them, are removed).
	* gaol/gaol_common.h: <iostream> with GAOL_DEBUGGING, without which
	gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h did not compile; debug_level is public.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h: the classes and functions of the
	expressions are public, which a shared libgaol did not export.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/gaol_interval.h: the
	constructor from two strings is gone: textToInterval(sl, sr) takes
	its place, and gives the empty set for bounds in the wrong order,
	where the constructor kept [2, 1].
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h: interval(double) is explicit: a
	double becomes an interval where the program writes interval(d).  The
	assignment, &= and |= of a double, and <, <=, > and >= between an
	interval and a double take interval(d), as the conversion did.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h: expression(double) and
	expression(const interval&) are explicit.
	* gaol/gaol_common.cpp (minimum, maximum): two zeros of different
	signs give -0 and +0 with Visual C++ too, which compiled the
	comparison as minsd and gave the other zero.
	* CMakeLists.txt: WITH_TESTS and WITH_EXAMPLES (GAOL_BUILD_TESTS
	before), BUILD_SHARED_LIBS, libgaol.so.5.0.0 as with configure and
	meson, GAOL_DEBUGGING in the Debug build, and the targets check,
	parser and perf.
	* gaol/Makefile.am: libgaol.so.5.0.0 (-version-info 5:0:0), where it
	was libgaol-5.0.so.0; the rules running flex and bison and the
	MAINTAINERCLEANFILES that deleted the committed lexer and parser are
	gone.
	* Makefile.am: the targets test, perf and parser.
	* meson.build, meson_options.txt, gaol/meson.build: with-tests and
	with-examples (with-test before); the build type gives the Debug
	build, and enable-optimize, enable-relations and check-perf are
	gone, enable-debug being a setting of the developers;
	libgaol.so.5.0.0; the targets check, perf and parser; the install
	with Visual C++ (the directory of gaol_configuration.h and
	gaol_fpu_msvc.h, a static library, no libm); the macros of the
	other builds, and no other.
	The headers of the meson files name Raphael Chenouard, who wrote
	them.
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build: the same options with
	the three builds: GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING, GAOL_SIMD (SSE2 on x86,
	NEON on 64-bit ARM, the FPU if it is off; GAOL_ARM64, --enable-arm64
	and enable-arm64 are gone), GAOL_COVERAGE (--enable-coverage,
	enable-coverage), WITH_TESTS, WITH_EXAMPLES, BUILD_SHARED_LIBS (the
	static library alone by default with configure and meson too, which
	made both), the build type, the position-independent code, and
	CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES (a universal build of macOS with configure
	and meson, by several -arch in the flags).  The fused multiply-add
	instructions and the AVX-512 path (taken with
	GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING) are decided by checks that compile, link
	and run a program, the 128-bit integer by gaol/gaol_u128.h and the
	debugging by the build type; none is an option any longer (the
	developers keep them as settings, the exceptions and the verbose
	mode too, which configure --help does not list and meson configure
	marks [developers]); in a cross-compilation the fused
	multiply-add instructions and the AVX-512 path are left out unless
	GAOL_MACHINE_RUNS_FMA, gaol_cv_machine_runs_fma or machine-runs-fma
	say that the machine runs them.  configure's --enable-optimize is
	gone.
	* configure.ac, 3rd/Makefile.am, meson.build, gaol/meson.build: the
	C sources of CORE-MATH are compiled with -std=gnu99, -msse2 -msse3
	and -fvisibility=hidden by the three builds; the audit compares the
	flags of one of them.
	* check/: moved into tests/, without CppUnit (tests/unit_tests.h);
	check/fpu.cpp, check/essai.cpp and the benchmark of GAOL 4 are gone.
	* tests/debugging.cpp: GAOL's headers with GAOL_DEBUGGING.
	* examples/: built by the three builds, and run by make check.
	* gaol/parser/regenerate_parser.sh: regenerates the lexer and the
	parser; the committed ones are generated by flex 2.6.4 and bison
	3.8.2, which give them again byte for byte.
	* doc/compare/code/run_perf.sh, results.csv, machine.txt: make perf
	measures GAOL v5 alone and writes the tables of performance.md.
	* .github: the continuous integration runs make test; shared
	library jobs and universal macOS jobs with the three builds, meson
	jobs with Visual C++, jobs for MSYS2 MINGW32, Cygwin, Clang for the
	target of Visual C++ and the generator of Xcode; the audit compares
	the configuration headers of the three builds.
	* Doxyfile, manual/gaol_doxygen.cfg.in, version.h, gaol.spec.in,
	stamp-h.in, mkinstalldirs: removed.
	* The lines $Id$ and "Last modified ... on pc-goualard" of the
	headers: removed.

	The builds, their options and the packages:

	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build, meson_options.txt,
	cmake/gaol_configuration.h.in, gaol/gaol_configuration.h.in,
	gaol/gaol_configuration_meson.h.in: GAOL_USING_ASM and its options
	(GAOL_ASM, --enable-asm, enable-asm) are gone, nothing reading them
	since f_negate() was removed; so is GAOL_HAVE_ROUNDING_MATH_OPTION
	(HAVE_ROUNDING_MATH_OPTION in GAOL 4), which nothing read.
	* gaol/Makefile.am, tests/Makefile.am, examples/Makefile.am,
	meson.build, gaol.pc.in, cmake/gaol.pc.in: no explicit -lm
	(LDADD, find_library('m'), Libs of gaol.pc): the C++ compiler
	links the math library, which GAOL still calls (<fenv.h>,
	sqrt, floor, fma...).
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build, the three
	gaol_configuration.h.in: a universal build of macOS computes its
	arm64 part with the NEON intervals and its x86_64 part with the
	SSE2 intervals (GAOL_UNIVERSAL_NEON, GAOL_UNIVERSAL_SSE2); the
	fused multiply-add instructions and the AVX-512 path stay off.
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build: Clang with the driver
	of GCC for the target of Visual C++ (_MSC_VER defined) is supported
	by the three builds: the libraries in C++14, which the standard
	library of Visual C++ needs, the unistd.h of cmake/msvc for the
	lexer, the static library alone (CMake and meson).
	* CMakeLists.txt: the generator of Xcode without
	CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES builds for the architecture of the
	machine; "arm64;x86_64" gives the universal build.
	* .github/scripts/tests.sh: the tests built with an installed GAOL
	get the Cflags of its gaol.pc, -mfma among them.
	* meson.build, gaol/meson.build: add_project_arguments() rather than
	add_global_arguments(), which meson refuses in a subproject; gaol_dep
	carries the flags of interval arithmetic and the link option of
	gaol.pc; no test setup nor target check in a subproject; the
	libraries in release in a subproject where meson before 1.8.4
	gives the debug of the project.  tests/meson_subproject and a job
	of build-systems.yml check it.
	* CMakeLists.txt: without a build type (add_subdirectory,
	FetchContent), gaol and gaol_core_math are compiled as in Release
	(-O3, NDEBUG, -funroll-loops...); with a generator of several
	configurations, Release comes first among them.
	tests/release_flags.py checks it on tests/fetch_content, in
	linux.yml.
	* CMakeLists.txt, meson_options.txt, meson.build: GAOL_DEBUG and
	enable-debug, settings of the developers, build the libraries of
	GAOL and CORE-MATH for debugging whatever the build type or the
	configuration, a project bringing GAOL in included.
	tests/release_flags.py --debug, the job build-types of linux.yml.
	* gaol/gaol_common.h, gaol/parser: the macro GAOL_DEBUG(lvl, cmd) is
	named GAOL_DEBUG_VERBOSE; GAOL_DEBUG is the setting of CMake.
	* CMakeLists.txt, cmake/gaol_version.cmake, meson.build,
	configure.ac: the three builds read VERSION.txt by the same
	rules: the six blanks of ASCII around the version and a UTF-8
	byte order mark at the start are ignored, a NUL byte is
	refused, UTF-16 is named in the message, which gives the first
	bytes of the file in hexadecimal; tests version_file and
	.github/scripts/version-file.sh.
	* CMakeLists.txt, cmake/gaol_package_source.cmake,
	tests/cpack_stale_configure.cmake: CMake warns, when it configures,
	that configure was generated for another version than VERSION.txt
	holds, which the archive of the sources (package_source) would
	carry; package_source configures the build directory again first
	after a change of VERSION.txt, with the Makefile generators too.
	* CMakeLists.txt: CPACK_VERBATIM_VARIABLES, so that CPack reads
	CPACK_SOURCE_IGNORE_FILES with its backslashes.
	* tests/cpack_stale_configure.cmake, tests/CMakeLists.txt: the
	compilers are passed with their arguments (CC="ccache gcc") and the
	program of the generator (a Ninja outside PATH); skipped without sh
	or symbolic links; checks package_source after a change of
	VERSION.txt.
	* .github/workflows/linux.yml: a job runs it with ccache in CC and
	Ninja outside PATH.
	* 3rd/Makefile.am, gaol/Makefile.am, tests/Makefile.am,
	examples/Makefile.am: distclean-local removes the .deps
	directories, which the Makefile.in of automake 1.18 empties and
	leaves, so that make distclean gives the sources back as git has
	them; the autotools jobs of build-systems.yml check it.
	* meson.build, gaol/meson.build: meson compiles the committed
	lexer and parser and no longer requires flex and bison; its
	generators, whose output nothing compiled, are gone.
	* doc/building.md, manual/v5/gaol.tex, meson.build: the Python
	that meson setup runs (the first of python3 and python found,
	then the one that runs meson) and the Microsoft Store aliases
	of Python (WindowsApps) that meson 0.53.0 and earlier may take
	for it.

	Compilers and platforms:

	* gaol/gaol_config.h: -ffast-math, -ffinite-math-only (which
	-ffast-math turns on), /fp:fast and Visual C++ without /fp:strict are
	refused; GAOL 4 refused none of them.  Clang's -Ofast and -ffast-math
	followed by -frounding-math are refused too (__FINITE_MATH_ONLY__ is
	1, __FAST_MATH__ is not defined).  The remedy is the flags of gaol.pc
	after the option: Clang's -ffast-math turns -frounding-math off,
	which -fno-fast-math does not turn on again.
	* tests/CMakeLists.txt, tests/refused_options.cpp,
	tests/refused_options.sh: the compile tests refused_finite_math_only,
	refused_fast_math and refused_positive, and the same checks with
	configure and meson.
	* .github/audit: __FINITE_MATH_ONLY__ is compared too.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h: clang-cl from Clang 16 is refused
	without /fp:strict, as Visual C++ is (tests/fp_strict checks
	it).
	* CMakeLists.txt, meson.build, gaol/meson.build: -msse2 for clang-cl
	on 32-bit x86, which computes doubles on the x87 unit even with
	/arch:SSE2; /Zc:strictStrings- for Visual C++ alone, clang-cl warning
	of it.
	* .github/workflows/windows.yml: jobs with clang-cl of Visual
	Studio 2022 (x64) and 2026 (x86, x64 and arm64).
	* gaol/gaol_port.h: GAOL_INFINITY is
	numeric_limits<double>::infinity(), where it was the HUGE_VAL
	of the C library, which the UCRT writes (double)(float)1e+300
	and clang-cl computes when the program runs: FLT_MAX in the
	downward and the toward-zero rounding.
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build: the C compiler, which
	builds CORE-MATH, is refused as the C++ compiler is: when it is Clang
	for a 32-bit ARM processor, or says of -frounding-math that the
	rounding direction is not honoured on the target (Clang 14 for 64-bit
	ARM); containers.yml and linux.yml check it for the three builds, for
	the C compiler alone, the C++ compiler alone and both.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h, tests/core_math.cpp: mingw-w64 is refused when
	the program gets mingw-w64's own fma() and round(), computed in
	doubles: that fma() is not correctly rounded, and CORE-MATH computes
	with it where the build has no -mfma; that round() depends on the
	rounding direction.  On x86-64, and on ARM for round() alone, those
	before version 12 and those linked with msvcrt.dll rather than the
	UCRT; on 32-bit x86, those before version 11 and, from 12 on, those
	linked with msvcrt.dll where the build has no -mfma.  The test checks
	first the fma() and round() of the C library in the four rounding
	directions, against exact rational arithmetic.
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build: GCC for Windows
	(MinGW-w64, MSYS2, Cygwin), on x86-64 and on 32-bit x86, is given
	-mfma -mprefer-vector-width=128 under the conditions of the other
	compilers, and a GCC without the second option none: -mfma alone
	lets GCC spill 256-bit registers to a stack it does not align (GCC
	bug 54412, fixed in GCC 17).
	.github/scripts/check-gcc-windows-fma.sh, run by windows.yml, fails
	on a line with %ymm in the objects of x86-64 (on 32-bit x86 it only
	reports them).  With MinGW-w64 15.2 for 32-bit x86, pow(x, 3) takes
	12 ns rather than 70, log 82 rather than 266 and cos 138 rather than
	490.  The library and the programs using it need a processor with the
	fused multiply-add instructions (Intel Haswell, AMD Piledriver and
	later); GAOL_FMA=OFF leaves them out, but MSYS2 MINGW32, which links
	msvcrt.dll, is then refused.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h: Cygwin is refused without -mfma, the fma() of
	its C library not being correctly rounded; the three builds give it
	-mfma, as they do to the other GCC for Windows.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp, CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac,
	meson.build: the exact products and the test of the machine that
	runs -mfma call __builtin_fma, the instruction with -mfma at every
	level of optimization, where std::fma without optimization is a
	call of the C library.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_msvc.h (previous_float,
	next_float): give -oo and +oo at -DBL_MAX and DBL_MAX with every C
	library; nextafter(-DBL_MAX, -oo) of Cygwin gave -DBL_MAX in the
	upward rounding, and the lower bound of sinh(-711) did not enclose
	its value.
	* gaol/gaol_port.h: _mm_malloc() and _mm_free() on Cygwin, whose
	<stdlib.h> does not declare posix_memalign() with -std=c++11.
	* .github/workflows: Cygwin x86-64 (GCC) is built and tested with
	the three builds.  package_source does not work there (CPack fails
	on the link README); tests/cpack_stale_configure.cmake says it is
	skipped.

	The installed headers and the names:

	* gaol/gaol_config.h: GAOL_PUBLIC replaces __GAOL_PUBLIC__, and
	is empty with Visual C++ and clang-cl, where GAOL is a static
	library: neither the builds, gaol::gaol, gaol.pc nor the code
	using GAOL define __GAOL_PUBLIC__= any longer, and a definition
	left changes nothing.
	* gaol/: the namespace gaol names the names of gaol_core one by
	one, rather than taking it whole: the names of GAOL 4 that GAOL
	v5 still has, the functions on intervals it adds,
	restore_rounding() and exact_string().  The functions that get
	and set the rounding_state (the rounding direction and the
	flush-to-zero modes), those that compute pow and the internals
	of GAOL 4 its manual did not document stay in gaol_core only
	(gaol_core::get_rounding()).
	* gaol/gaol_allocator.h: removed, with gaol::aligned_allocator.
	* gaol/: no cast of C in the installed headers (static_cast;
	reinterpret_cast and const_cast in GAOL_HI() and GAOL_LO()),
	which .github/scripts/headers.sh checks with -Wold-style-cast.
	* gaol/gaol_port.h, gaol/gaol_config.h,
	gaol/gaol_configuration.h.in, configure.ac, CMakeLists.txt,
	meson.build: every macro the headers define starts with GAOL_
	or gaol_ (the former INLINE, MEMALIGN, __HI, __LO, HAVE_FENV_H,
	USING_SSE2_INSTRUCTIONS, IX86_LINUX, WORDS_BIGENDIAN,
	ULONGLONGINT...); _XOPEN_SOURCE is no longer redefined.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h: nb_fp_numbers() returns an
	unsigned long long; interval& operator=(const interval&) =
	default, so that -Wextra does not warn at each assignment.
	* gaol/gaol_exceptions.h: no using std::exception nor using
	std::string in the global namespace.
	* gaol/Makefile.am, CMakeLists.txt, gaol/meson.build: the same
	list of installed headers with the three builds, without those
	of the sources (parser/gaol_interval_parser.h,
	gaol_init_cleanup.h, gaol_core_math.h, gaol_u128.h).
	* .github/scripts/headers.sh: each installed header but four
	compiles alone with -Wall -Wextra -Wold-style-cast -Wundef
	-Werror, and the macros of gaol/gaol all start with GAOL_ or
	gaol_; linux.yml: a job compiles GAOL, the tests and the
	examples with -Wall -Wextra -Werror.
	* gaol/gaol_port.h: the doubles two_pi, pi, half_pi, pi_dn,
	pi_up, half_pi_dn, half_pi_up, ln2_dn, ln2_up, two_power_51
	and two_power_52 are no longer declared for the code using
	GAOL, which found them with using namespace gaol (a pi of its
	own was ambiguous); the bounds of pi, pi/2 and 2pi are those
	of interval::pi(), half_pi() and two_pi(); the four doubles of
	pi and pi/2 the library uses are gaol_pi_dn, gaol_pi_up,
	gaol_half_pi_dn and gaol_half_pi_up, in gaol_core.
	* gaol/gaol_port.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h,
	gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp: the constants of GAOL
	(gaol_pi_dn..., the static intervals interval::cst_*, which
	are gone, and the masks of the SSE2 intervals) are initialized
	when compiling, so that the intervals computed in the
	initialization of a static object of a program are right;
	tests/static_initialization.cpp.
	* cmake/gaol_configuration.h.in, gaol/gaol_configuration.h.in,
	gaol/gaol_configuration_meson.h.in: the boolean options are defined
	to 0 when the build leaves them undefined.  gaol/gaol_config.h,
	gaol/gaol_fpu.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h: the macros of the target are
	defined to 0 where they do not hold.  The installed headers read
	them with #if, and compile under -Wundef -Werror.
	* gaol/parser/: the expressions (gaol_expression.h, gaol_expr_eval.h,
	gaol_expr_visitor.h, gaol_eval_stack.h), the reader of strings
	(gaol_parser.h), the lexer, the parser and regenerate_parser.sh,
	moved from gaol/; the three builds install the five headers in
	include/gaol/parser/, 23 headers in all with those of include/gaol/
	and include/gaol/core/.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp:
	the class interval, the functions of gaol_core and the code of the
	SSE2, NEON and FPU intervals, split from gaol/gaol_interval.h and
	gaol/gaol_interval.cpp, which keep the namespace gaol and
	textToInterval().
	* CMakeLists.txt, gaol/Makefile.am, gaol/meson.build:
	gaol_interval_core.h, gaol_interval_fpu.h, gaol_interval_neon.h
	and gaol_interval_sse.h are installed in include/gaol/core/, as
	their sources are in gaol/core/, rather than in include/gaol/
	(GAOL 4 installed gaol_interval_fpu.h and gaol_interval_sse.h
	there); gaol_interval.h includes gaol_interval_core.h.
	* gaol/float/: gaol_intervalf.*, gaol_interval2f.* and
	gaol_generator_mul_i2f.py, moved from gaol/; none is installed.
	* gaol/gaol_parameters.h: removed, it declared an empty namespace.
	* gaol/gaol_limits.h: removed, it only included <limits>.
	* gaol/gaol_assert.h: removed, with GAOL_ASSERT, which was assert()
	with GAOL_DEBUGGING; the Debug builds keep assert() active, and
	GAOL_DEBUGGING only turns on GAOL_DEBUG_VERBOSE.
	* gaol/gaol_profile.h, gaol/gaol_profile.cpp: removed: the class
	timepiece and get_time(), reset_time(), elapsed_time() and
	intermediate_elapsed_time() leave the interface, and the macro
	HAVE_GETRUSAGE with them.
	* gaol/gaol_exact.h, gaol/gaol_exact.c, gaol/sysdeps/: removed, with
	get_inexact() and clear_inexact(), which the reader of numbers called
	to know whether strtod() had rounded and which numbers read exactly
	do not need.
	* gaol/gaol_flags.h, gaol/s_nextafter.c: removed; the class prec_t
	of gaol_flags.h is in gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h.
	* gaol/dllmain.cpp: removed, no build compiled it.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_msvc.h, gaol/gaol_port.h:
	f_negate(), f_negate_simple(), the macro opposite(), get_fpu_cw()
	and reset_fpu_cw() are removed; GAOL v5 no longer calls them.

	The kinds of intervals:

	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_neon.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_neon.cpp:
	the intervals of 64-bit ARM computed with NEON, the two bounds in a
	float64x2_t as the SSE2 intervals keep them in a __m128d (GAOL_SIMD,
	GAOL_USING_NEON_INSTRUCTIONS): +, -, *, /, sqr and sqrt are kernels
	over registers, the product the maximum of four products with no
	branch on the signs, and no double is compared on bounds that may be
	NaN.  The sign of a zero bound can differ from that of the SSE2 and FPU
	intervals ([-1, 0] * [-1, 0] has the lower bound -0).
	* gaol/gaol_fpu.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h: on 64-bit ARM with the NEON
	intervals the rounding direction (the field RMode of FPCR) is read
	with mrs, which raises no flag, and written with msr only when it is
	not upward or a flush mode is set, where the FPU intervals compute
	1 + (2^-1060 + 0) to see it; with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING an operation
	reads FPCR once and writes it back only if it differs (27 ns per
	operation rather than 39 on an Apple M2).
	* gaol/gaol_config.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h, CMakeLists.txt,
	configure.ac, meson.build: the NEON intervals with every compiler
	of the 64-bit ARM processors: Visual C++ (FPCR read and written
	with _ReadStatusReg() and _WriteStatusReg()), clang-cl and the
	Clang of MinGW-w64 and MSYS2, as GCC and Clang; the operations
	clear FZ and FIZ of FPCR with Visual C++ too.
	* CMakeLists.txt, meson.build: Visual C++ and clang-cl for x64 compute
	the intervals with SSE2, and take the AVX-512 path where the machine
	building runs it and GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING is on.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp, gaol/core/gaol_interval_avx512.cpp,
	gaol/gaol_common.cpp: written for the __m128d of Visual C++ (no vector
	initializers, no asm statement, no target attribute), the operations
	of the AVX-512 path ordered there by volatile accesses and calls;
	cpu_runs_avx512f() tells the processor by cpuid and xgetbv.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.h,
	gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp: GAOL_VECTORCALL (__vectorcall with
	Visual C++ and clang-cl for x64) on add_bounds() and the other kernels
	of the SSE2 intervals, whose __m128d arguments go in registers;
	left_internal() and right_internal() read the register with Visual
	C++; mid() keeps the halves with a negated factor as written.
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build, cmake/gaol.pc.in,
	gaol/gaol_config.h: the SSE2 intervals on 32-bit Windows
	with GCC and Clang (MinGW-w64, MSYS2).  The C runtime aligns new on 8
	bytes and GCC takes it to be aligned on 16, so that a std::vector of
	intervals crashed on its first movaps; -faligned-new=8, given to the
	library and, by gaol::gaol, gaol.pc and the dependency of meson, to
	the code using it, makes the compiler ask for aligned memory, and
	gaol_config.h refuses the SSE2 intervals without it.  A GCC or Clang
	that takes no such flag keeps the FPU intervals.  tests/misc.cpp
	(test_heap_alignment), tests/refused_aligned_new.cpp,
	tests/refused_options.sh.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h, gaol/gaol_fpu.h,
	gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac,
	meson.build: Visual C++ and clang-cl compute with the SSE2
	intervals on 32-bit Windows, the two bounds being two doubles of
	alignment 8 loaded with movupd, which need no flag asking for
	aligned memory (GAOL_SSE2_UNALIGNED_STORAGE); __vectorcall for the
	kernels; with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING, the x87 control word is set
	as well as MXCSR.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_avx512.cpp: the AVX-512 path: x + y,
	x - y, x * y, x / y, sqrt(x), sqr(x), the same four with a double
	and, with FMA, the integer powers beyond 1 and -1 are computed with
	the rounding the AVX-512 instructions carry; they set no rounding
	direction and raise no exception flag, and their bounds are those
	of the SSE2 intervals, but for the sign of a zero bound of x * d.
	The three builds take the path with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING when the
	machine building runs the instructions; gaol::init() stops a
	program run on a processor without them.  GCC for Windows never
	takes the path (GCC bug 54412).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp: +,
	-, * and / of two SSE2 intervals and sqr() of one take the bounds of
	their operands in a register and give those of the result in one,
	from functions of the library; *, / and sqr() no longer branch on the
	signs of the bounds.  The sign of a zero bound of a product or of a
	quotient may differ from GAOL 4's: [-1, 0] * [-1, 0] has the lower
	bound +0.
	* doc/compare/code/run_lines.sh, bench_ops.h: ten long lines of
	operators, with their tables at the end of doc/compare/performance.md.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_fpu.cpp: the FPU intervals (GAOL_SIMD=OFF,
	32-bit ARM, POWER, s390x, RISC-V) compute the product, the quotient,
	the remainder and the square without a branch on the signs of the
	bounds, as the SSE2 and the NEON intervals do, where GAOL 4 took one
	of nine cases of the signs for a product and went down up to eight
	tests for a quotient: a product of random signs takes 3.5 ns, where
	it took 13.9 (Apple M2, doc/compare).  A line of products of operands
	of one sign takes more time (35.8 ns for seven, where it took 23.8).
	The sign of a zero bound of a product is not specified.  A product
	forms every corner, as with SSE2 and NEON: a rounded subnormal corner
	that is no bound of the result raises FE_UNDERFLOW, and a corner that
	overflows FE_OVERFLOW, the result then having an infinite bound;
	tests/arithmetic.cpp.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (max, min): the SSE2 and NEON
	intervals take both bounds of the result at once, from one
	comparison of the stored bounds, where maximum() and minimum() were
	called for each bound: 3 to 4 times faster on x86-64, 1.5 to 2.4
	times on 64-bit ARM, the same bounds bit for bit.  With the SSE2
	intervals, a signaling NaN in a stored bound raises the
	invalid-operation exception with Clang too, as it did with GCC.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol_interval_sse.h,
	gaol_interval_neon.h: interval(const __m128d&) and
	interval(const float64x2_t&) give the empty set for the lanes of
	[+oo] and [-oo], which hold no real number and which no other
	constructor makes (sqr([+oo]) was [+oo]), and are explicit;
	interval(register, gaol_detail::unchecked_bounds) is the
	constructor without the check, for the code of GAOL.

	The rounding direction and the control registers:

	* gaol/gaol_common.h: gaol::restore_rounding() sets back the
	rounding direction that the first gaol::init() found, as many
	times as the program needs, where gaol::cleanup() does so at its
	first call only; it does nothing with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu.h: the modes flushing subnormals to zero (FTZ
	and DAZ on x86, FZ on ARM) are detected by the check of each
	operation that computes bounds (1 + (2^-1060 + 0), or a read of the
	control register where GAOL reads it) and cleared; the check comes
	before the bounds are read or compared, and the result is made before
	the modes are set back with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING;
	tests/rounding_direction.cpp compares each operation, under each mode
	and direction, with its result with the modes cleared.
	* CMakeLists.txt, configure.ac, meson.build,
	tests/fast_math_link.cpp: -mno-daz-ftz is given to the link of
	the code using GAOL where the compiler accepts it (gaol.pc,
	gaol::gaol).
	* gaol/gaol_port.h (bound_less, bound_less_equal, bound_greater,
	bound_greater_equal, bound_equal, bound_key, bound_bits,
	bound_is_subnormal, bound_floor, bound_ceil, bound_trunc,
	bound_round): new.  The functions that make no check of the rounding
	direction compare the bounds by their bits where they compare equal,
	and round a subnormal bound from its bits, so that the flush-to-zero
	and denormals-are-zero modes change none of their results: the
	constructor from two bounds, &=, |=, the relations, sign, floor, ceil,
	integer, trunc, round_ties_to_away (gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h and
	the SSE2, NEON and FPU intervals of gaol/core/), maximum and minimum
	(gaol/gaol_common.cpp, hence max, min, abs, mag), mig, smig,
	is_canonical, feven, nb_fp_numbers (gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp),
	inf and sup (gaol/gaol_ieee1788.h).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (midpoint, chi, operator<<): the
	check of the rounding direction, which clears these modes, before the
	bounds are read; set back with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING.
	(number_to_text): where GAOL cannot clear these modes, a subnormal
	bound written outward, as 2^-1022 or 0.  (is_canonical): the double
	above the left bound from its bits: nextafter() of the UCRT and of
	macOS is wrong under denormals-are-zero.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_avx512.cpp (flush_guard): the operands read
	after the modes are cleared, the results made before they are set back
	with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING: Clang 18 computed x - y after.  The
	embedded rounding honours flush-to-zero.
	* .github/workflows/linux.yml: Clang on x86_64 with
	GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING.
	* tests/rounding_direction.cpp: the functions that make no check, and
	those that do, under each mode.  tests/numbers.cpp (subnormal_output):
	the text written under denormals-are-zero is the one written without
	it.  tests/performance.cpp: x | y, max, midpoint, and the time of the
	check with 2^-1060 and with 2^-60.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp
	(is_bisectable, bisect): the bounds told apart by their bits, so
	that the results are the same whatever the flush-to-zero modes
	(denormals-are-zero, FZ) of the program.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h: on 32-bit x86, the control
	word of the x87 unit and MXCSR are read in line (fnstcw, stmxcsr)
	instead of through fegetround(), a call of about 80 ns with Visual
	C++, and only the register that is not upward is written (fldcw,
	ldmxcsr); with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING an operation reads the
	registers it sets once and writes back what differs from what it
	found, and gaol::cleanup() and gaol::restore_rounding() set each unit
	back to its own direction.  tests/rounding_direction.cpp checks the
	decisions on simulated registers.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu.h: on x86-64 GAOL reads and writes the control
	registers itself, with every compiler, and only the one that differs,
	not through fegetround() and fesetround() (x.midpoint() takes 17.5 ns
	rather than 27.9 with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING): with mingw-w64 13 and
	later, fesetround() clears the six exception flags of MXCSR when it
	changes it, and fegetround() joins by OR the rounding fields of the
	x87 unit and of MXCSR.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h: on x86, with
	GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING, the SSE2 operations read MXCSR once, write
	it at their start only if the direction is not upward or a mode
	flushing the subnormals to zero is set, and at their end only if
	it differs from what they found (sse_enter_upward(), sse_leave());
	round_upward(), round_nearest() and round_downward() write the x87
	control word and MXCSR only where their direction differs, and no
	other bit.  x + y takes 1.7 ns rather than 8.2 in a program that
	computes upward.
	* gaol/gaol_fpu.h: with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING, Visual C++ for x64
	with /arch:AVX2 writes MXCSR alone in each operation, as GCC, Clang
	and clang-cl do with __FMA__: GAOL_RND_ENTER() and
	GAOL_RND_LEAVE() called fegetround() and fesetround() of the C
	runtime there, and pow(x, 3), sqrt(), exp(), log(), sin() and cos()
	take 60 to 64 ns less (AMD EPYC runners of GitHub).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp
	(width, bisect): the bounds go through rnd_reread() after the check of
	the rounding direction.  Built in the calling code they are registers,
	which GCC (and Clang on 64-bit ARM) subtracted before the control
	register was written: with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING, and a program not
	computing upward, the width of [1, 2^53 + 2] was 2^53;
	tests/rounding_direction.cpp.
	* gaol/gaol_config.h, gaol/gaol_fpu.h, gaol/gaol_fpu_fenv.h: on 32-bit
	ARM with GCC and a floating-point unit of doubles, FPSCR is read with
	vmrs and written with vmsr, only when it changes, rather than through
	fegetround() and fesetround(): with GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING x + y takes
	22.5 ns, where it took 48 (GAOL_RND_FPSCR_DIRECT,
	-DGAOL_RND_FPSCR_DIRECT=0 gives the C library back).  Without
	GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING nothing changes.

	Floating-point exceptions:

	* gaol/: no operation, relation, function or output raises the
	invalid-operation exception for the empty set: quiet comparisons
	(std::islessequal(), std::isunordered()...) in is_empty(), &=,
	set_contains(), set_disjoint(), set_leq(), straddles_zero(),
	is_canonical(), chi(), the output and the first comparison of the
	constructors, which gives the empty set for NaN bounds (floor, ceil,
	integer, split, textToInterval(sl, sr)), and the empty set told
	before them in sqrt, exp, modulo_k_pi and, with the SSE2 and NEON
	intervals, min and max; the unary minus of the FPU intervals
	exchanges the bounds.  Nor do they for a NaN the program
	gives (interval(NAN), x += NAN, x <= NAN), for the SSE2 product of a
	zero bound by an infinite one, for the SSE2 div_rel with infinite
	bounds, nor for pow with an exponent of extreme magnitude: a program
	that enabled the exception (feenableexcept(FE_INVALID)) no longer
	dies on SIGFPE there.  midpoint() of [DBL_MAX] raises no overflow
	exception.  With Visual C++ for x64, and for x86 with SSE2, the quiet
	comparisons are ucomisd in line, not calls to the C library.
	doc/using.md lists what is raised, and tests/rounding_direction.cpp
	checks it with the exceptions enabled in a child process (glibc).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/core/gaol_interval_fpu.h: on
	32-bit ARM (GCC 12 to 14) and POWER9, is_empty() and the constructors
	of the FPU intervals tell NaN bounds with std::isunordered() first,
	GCC having compiled its quiet comparisons into signaling ones.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp, gaol_interval_fpu.cpp,
	gaol_interval_sse.cpp, gaol_interval_neon.cpp: no overflow nor
	division-by-zero exception for a result with no infinite bound,
	the empty set included, from sqrt, nth_root, sin, cos, tan, atan
	(Visual C++), sqrt_rel, nth_root_rel, div_rel, atan_rel,
	atan2_rel_x, atan2_rel_y, atanh_rel, pow_rel and
	pow_exponent_rel: a quotient, a product or a power that would
	overflow is replaced by what the operation gives, without being
	computed, the roots of an argument above 2^1000 are proved on
	scaled values, and sin and cos take the half of the width.  The
	bounds are the same, up to the sign of a zero bound of div_rel
	with the SSE2 intervals.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (two_sum): cancel_minus() and
	cancel_plus() raise no invalid-operation exception when a difference
	of the bounds is beyond DBL_MAX, nor when a sum next to DBL_MAX is
	halfway between two doubles and rounds away from the exact one; the
	error of the sum is computed by the Fast2Sum of Dekker, the operand of
	the larger magnitude first, which cannot overflow, and an infinite sum
	has no error.  cancel_minus([DBL_MAX - 2^971, DBL_MAX], [2^970,
	3*2^970]) was [-oo, +oo]; tests/rounding_direction.cpp.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp (/, %, inverse): the SSE2 intervals
	divide only the half of a register that a branch keeps, so that a
	divisor with a zero bound (1/[0, 1], [1, 2]/[0, 2], [1, 2]%[0, 2],
	inverse([0, 2]), pow([0, 2], -1)) raises no division-by-zero
	exception.  doc/using.md, manual/v5/gaol.tex: 1/[0, 1] is no example
	of the division-by-zero exception any more.

	CORE-MATH and the elementary functions:

	* gaol/core_math_port.h: on x86, 32-bit and 64-bit, the sources of
	CORE-MATH read the rounding direction from MXCSR, the register of
	their doubles, not from the x87 unit nor through the fegetround() of
	the C library, which cbrt and asinpi called at every call on 32-bit
	x86; pow missed subnormal results when the two units differed
	(tests/rounding_direction.cpp).  The square root is that of the SSE2
	instructions on every 32-bit x86, because the one of the C libraries
	of Windows rounds to nearest in every direction; NAN and INFINITY
	are read from their bits with Visual C++ (pow rounded downward was
	-0 for tiny results).
	* 3rd/math-core/src/binary64/cbrt/cbrt.c, rsqrt/rsqrt.c,
	asinpi/asinpi.c: get_rounding_mode() takes the rounding field
	of MXCSR as 0 to 3 whatever the FE_* values of the C library,
	which it took wrongly on x86-64 with clang-cl and Cygwin (the
	three) and with mingw-w64 before 13 (cbrt): the bound of
	nth_root(x, 3)
	farther from zero and the upper bounds of rsqrt and asinpi did
	not enclose (tests/core_math.cpp).
	* 3rd/math-core/src/binary64/pow/pow.c: exact_pow() converts
	its 54-bit integer in two exact halves on 32-bit ARM, where
	__aeabi_l2d rounds to nearest in every direction: the upper
	bound of pow([3*2^-30], [34, 35]) was below the power.
	* 3rd/math-core/src/binary64/pow/pow.c (exp_1): RHO2 lowered to
	0x1.62e42d29db3f0p+9, the largest rh with rh * INVLOG2 rounded below
	2^22: from there to the former RHO2 the first phase scaled its
	approximation by 2^1024 and computed Inf - Inf, which raised the
	invalid-operation exception for a power next to the largest double
	(pow([2], [1023.9999])); tests in tests/core_math.cpp and
	tests/rounding_direction.cpp; 3rd/README.md: item 9 and patch 8 to
	propose to CORE-MATH.
	* 3rd/README.md: the fixes of the vendored CORE-MATH sources,
	written as patches against CORE-MATH's master to propose
	upstream (the masks of a 64-bit long, the shift of asinpi_acc,
	the __builtin_expect of rsqrt, the __builtin_roundeven of sin,
	the rounding direction of pow, the rounding field of MXCSR in
	cbrt, rsqrt and asinpi, pow on 32-bit ARM, pow next to the
	overflow), of which upstream has made the rsqrt and sin ones
	since; glibc is checked for the same defects.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp: the smallest subnormal double
	is a literal, where GCC converted
	std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min() from long double at
	each use with -frounding-math (a microcode assist on Intel
	processors): sqrt() took 124 ns, it takes 7.8 ns (GCC 15.2, Intel
	i7-1185G7).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (tan): an interval whose
	width rounded upward is at most the double below pi is
	narrower than pi, so tan([-M_PI_2, M_PI_2]) is [-1.63e16,
	1.63e16], no longer [-oo, +oo]; tests/elementary.cpp: tan next
	to two consecutive poles, widths about the double below pi.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (atanh): the empty set for
	an interval with no point of (-1, 1), atanh([1]) having been
	[MAX, +oo]; atanh_rel and tanhRev follow; tests in
	tests/elementary.cpp and tests/reverse_mappings.cpp.

	Powers and roots:

	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp: pow(x, n) and
	gaol_ieee1788::pown(x, n) give the tightest bounds of x^n, of either
	sign, or one double beyond where x^n is within twice the error bound
	of a double (2^-98 relatively at n = 3, never for n = 2, which is
	sqr(x)): both bounds at once from exact products, with an error bound
	proved beforehand; a corner whose powers leave [2^-968, 2^960] is
	computed alone and scaled.  Beyond the ints, the pow of CORE-MATH at
	the corners (gaol_pown_ll).
	* README.md, doc/using.md, manual/v5: the two namespaces, gaol for
	GAOL 4 and gaol_ieee1788 for IEEE 1788-2015, and their powers side by
	side.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp: pow(x, [n]), pow(x, n.0) and
	gaol_ieee1788::pow(x, [n]) for an integer n, nth_root(x, q) for q < 0,
	acosh_rel(), asinh_rel(), atanh_rel() and nth_root_rel() check the
	rounding direction once instead of twice, the bodies they call
	(pown_upward(), nth_root_upward()...) no longer checking it; the
	bounds are the same, bit for bit.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (modulo_k_pi): one check of the
	rounding direction, and the quotients by pi computed on doubles, as
	tan() computes its own: 10 ns instead of 22; a bound 0 gives a
	quotient 0 of its own sign, an infinite bound NaN.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (nth_root): |q| is computed without
	a signed overflow for q = INT_MIN, where long has 32 bits.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (pow): pow(x, y) is taken at the
	corners of every box, and exp(y log x) is gone: at a corner where x is
	0 or +oo, or y infinite, the bound is the limit of x^y, 0 or +oo (1
	for x = 1 or y = 0), so that pow([4, +oo], 0.5) is [2, +oo], and such
	boxes no longer raise the division-by-zero exception.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h (nth_root): overloads for an order of
	type long long and unsigned long long, which check the rounding
	direction once beyond the unsigned ints.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h (rnd_keep): with
	GAOL_PRESERVE_ROUNDING, rnd_keep() of an interval keeps its two
	bounds, instead of copying the interval a byte at a time into volatile
	memory: pow(x, [-3]) and nth_root_rel() 26 % faster.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp: fast_modulo_k_pi(), which tan()
	no longer calls and no header declares, is removed.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/gaol_ieee1788.h,
	gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h: an integer of any type but bool is
	taken as the integer it is where GAOL took a double: interval(n)
	is the tightest interval containing n, so that interval(2^53+1) is
	[2^53, 2^53+2], where the conversion to a double gave a point that
	is not n; so do interval(a, b) with an integer bound, which is
	empty for a > b compared as numbers, the assignments, the
	arithmetic operators, the relations, set_contains(),
	expression(n), numsToInterval() and isMember().  pow(x, n) and
	pown(x, n) take an exponent of any integer type, where an exponent
	beyond the ints was converted to an int.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h, gaol/gaol_ieee1788.h,
	gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h: nth_root(x, q), rootn(x, q),
	pownRev(c, x, p) and the pow(e, n), pown(e, n) and nth_root(e, n)
	of the expressions take an integer of any type, as pow(x, n)
	does; an order beyond the unsigned ints is enclosed through pow
	with the exponent 1/q, and nth_root(e, -2) is no longer the
	order 4294967294.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp: pow(x, y) with an interval
	exponent, pow(x, d) with a double, pow(x, n) with a long long
	beyond the unsigned ints and gaol_ieee1788::pow(x, y) return the
	empty set for an empty operand before they check the rounding
	direction, as sqr() does: the direction and the modes that flush
	the subnormals to zero stay as the program had them.  The integer
	powers other than 2, of an int or an unsigned, still check first.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (nth_root): the proofs of the
	bounds of the roots of an x below 2^-1020 take scaled powers, which
	are never subnormal: nth_root([2^-1074], 33) was 1983 doubles below
	its tightest lower bound and 28785 above its upper one, and
	nth_root(x, q) for q < 0 followed.  The bounds are the tightest, or up
	to 2 doubles beyond (4 for q < 0), for every x, where the
	documentation said one double; tests/arithmetic.cpp.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (pow): at the corner of the
	box that gives the lower bound, where x^y is a double, the
	lower bound is x^y itself instead of the double below
	CORE-MATH's value; pow_is_double() proves it exactly from the
	bits of x and y.  pow([4], 0.5) is [2].

	Functions of intervals:

	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h (width_enclosure, inflate, midrad,
	bisect, is_bisectable, hull, intersect): new, the tools that
	algorithms of intervals write for themselves, each bound rounded
	outward.  bisect throws invalid_action_error for a ratio outside
	(0, 1) or a NaN.  Named in gaol.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h (erf, erfc): new, the tightest
	bounds, with the erf and erfc of CORE-MATH (3rd/math-core).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h (pow_rel, pow_exponent_rel,
	atan2_rel_y, atan2_rel_x, max_rel, min_rel, sign_rel, floor_rel),
	gaol/gaol_ieee1788.h (powRev1, powRev2, atan2Rev1, atan2Rev2,
	mulRevToPair): new.  Of the reverse operations of IEEE 1788-2015, only
	pownRev for p <= 0 is missing.
	* gaol/gaol_ldexp.h, gaol/core_math_port.h: CORE-MATH calls
	gaol_ldexp() with every compiler, x*2^n rounded once in the direction
	in effect: erfc rounds its subnormal results with ldexp(), which the C
	libraries of Windows and macOS do not round that way.
	* doc/compare/: ITF1788 (itf1788/, itf1788.md), the benchmark of Tang
	et al. 2021 (tang2021/, tang2021.md), Boost.Interval, and a test of
	the enclosure of the elementary functions (enclosure.md).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp, gaol_interval_fpu.cpp,
	gaol_interval_neon.cpp (hausdorff): equal bounds, infinite
	ones included, are at distance 0 (hausdorff([1,+oo],[2,+oo])
	is 1, not +oo); gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp
	(nb_fp_numbers): the doubles are numbered by the bits of |a|
	and |b|, so -0 is +0 (nb_fp_numbers(-0.0, 1.0) was
	13830554455654793217).
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_sse.cpp, gaol/core/gaol_interval_fpu.cpp,
	gaol/core/gaol_interval_neon.cpp (hausdorff): +oo returned before
	the check of the rounding direction when a bound is infinite in one
	interval only.
	* tests/reverse_mappings.cpp: atanh_rel compared at its infinite
	ends.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.h (lexicographic_less): new, a total
	order on the intervals for std::set, std::map and std::sort, which <
	is not (it is strictPrecedes): the empty set first, then the intervals
	by their left bound and by their right bound.  Named in gaol
	(gaol/gaol_interval.h).
	* gaol/gaol_config.h (GAOL_NODISCARD): the attribute of the
	functions whose result is all they do, so that sqrt(x); gets a
	warning.  It is [[nodiscard]] from C++17 and, before it, where
	__has_cpp_attribute(nodiscard) says the compiler takes it (GCC 7
	and later, Visual C++ 2019 16.4 and later),
	__attribute__((warn_unused_result)) with Clang and GCC before 7,
	and _Check_return_ of <sal.h> with older Visual C++;
	tests/nodiscard.cpp: the compile tests nodiscard_discard_cxx11, 14
	and 17 and nodiscard_used_cxx11, 14 and 17.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (chi): GAOL_NAN for the
	empty set, as width() returns; the quotient of the NaN bounds
	was -nan with GCC and nan with Clang.

	Reading numbers, intervals and strings:

	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_parser.ypp (gaol_lookup_function): the
	names of functions are read in any case, compared in ASCII and not by
	std::tolower(), whose result depends on the locale: under a Turkish
	locale, SIN(...) would have been refused.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (operator>>): the blanks before the
	line, line ends included, are skipped, under std::noskipws too: a
	blank line between two intervals, or at the end of the input, no
	longer throws input_format_error, and in >> d >> x reads two values
	written on two lines.
	* tests/numbers.cpp, tests/gaol_tests.h: the reading and the writing
	of subnormals are checked under the modes that flush them to zero on
	64-bit ARM (FZ, FIZ) and 32-bit ARM (FZ) too, not on x86 only.
	* tests/expressions.cpp: the names of functions are read under a
	locale where std::tolower('I') is not 'i'.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_parser.ypp (gaol_operation): the
	operations + - * / are computed when they are read, as the calls of
	functions are; the parser keeps a tree only for a chain of minus
	signs.  gaol/parser/gaol_expr_eval.h, gaol_eval_stack.h,
	gaol_expression.cpp (release_node): the evaluation of an expression
	goes by recursion to 32 levels, then by a loop over a stack of its own
	in the heap, and the destruction of a node by a loop: a sum of 100000
	terms, read from a string or built with gaol/parser/gaol_expression.h,
	overflowed the stack.  eval_stack and expr_eval are no longer
	copyable.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp, gaol_interval_parser.ypp
	(gaol_parse_string): the lexer reads the string by YY_INPUT, with its
	length: a std::string is read whole, a NUL character is no character
	of any token, and a string of more than 2^32 characters is no longer
	read as its first ones.  gaol_ieee1788::textToInterval() is compiled
	in the library (gaol/gaol_ieee1788.cpp), where it was inline.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp, gaol_interval_parser.ypp:
	YY_FATAL_ERROR throws std::bad_alloc, where flex called exit(2); every
	action catches what it throws and the parser frees its nodes, and
	flex's blocks are freed, after an error;
	gaol_ieee1788::textToInterval() catches gaol_exception alone, and
	std::bad_alloc passes.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_parser.ypp (GAOL_PARSING_ERROR): without
	exceptions, a wrong call in a string (pown([2,5],2.5)) aborts the
	reading as a syntax error does, where it called abort():
	gaol_ieee1788::textToInterval() gives the empty set, parse_interval()
	returns false.  (gaol_literal): the two numbers of <a, b> are compared
	by their bits.
	* tests/expressions.cpp (deep_strings, nul_characters,
	allocation_failures), tests/numbers.cpp (degenerate_subnormals),
	tests/ieee1788.cpp: long and deeply nested strings, NUL characters,
	every allocation made to fail in turn, <a, b> of subnormals under
	denormals-are-zero, and wrong calls without exceptions.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp: a number above the
	largest double (1e400, 1.8e308, 0x1p1024) or below the least
	normal one (1e-310, 1e-400) is no longer given to strtod(),
	which raised FE_OVERFLOW or FE_UNDERFLOW for it (1e400*0 raised
	the flag of an infinite bound): it is compared with the largest
	double (with 2^-1022) first, and the search for the doubles
	around it starts from there (from 0).  Reading a subnormal
	takes 2 to 12 times longer.  The exponent and the counts of
	digits are long long, where a long of 32 bits wrapped for a
	text of more than 536870411 characters.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_parser.ypp (gaol_literal): <a, b>
	refuses a bound whose value is not a point: <[0.1],
	0x1.9999999999999p-4> was read as the point of the second.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_parser.ypp (gaol_literal): [l, u] with l
	above u is the empty set ([9007199254740993, 9007199254740992] was
	[2^53], [1.00000000000000001, 1] was [1]); a number that is not a
	double is a leaf (inside_node) strictly inside its interval, which
	holds the rank of its text; two such numbers between the same doubles
	are compared exactly (gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp:
	compare_numbers, gaol_natural_mul); <a, b> refuses a bound that is no
	double (<0.1, 0x1.9999999999999p-4> was [0x1.9999999999999p-4]); tests
	in tests/expressions.cpp.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_parser.ypp (gaol_call): the second
	argument of nth_root, pown and rootn is a long long, saturated to
	+-(2^63 - 2) from 2^63 on: nth_root(8,3000000000) was refused; test in
	tests/expressions.cpp.
	* gaol/gaol_interval.h, gaol/gaol_interval.cpp,
	gaol/gaol_ieee1788.h, gaol/gaol_ieee1788.cpp: textToInterval(s),
	textToInterval(sl, sr) and, in gaol_ieee1788, textToInterval(s)
	and exactToInterval(s) have overloads for a const char*: a null
	pointer throws input_format_error, where the std::string built
	from it has undefined behaviour (std::logic_error with
	libstdc++).  The address of any of them needs a cast or a typed
	pointer.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_parser.cpp (parse_interval): a null pointer is
	refused, false being returned, where strlen() read it and the program
	died of a segmentation fault; test in tests/expressions.cpp.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp: numbers are read right
	whatever the flush-to-zero and denormals-are-zero modes (0 is
	0, the subnormals are enclosed tightly): the reader takes the
	doubles apart by their bits.
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp: a number is taken apart
	once for the comparisons of gaol_enclose_number(), so that a
	long number reads in about the time it takes under the C
	locale when the locale writes a decimal comma (20000
	characters: over 2 s down to about 0.02 s).
	* gaol/parser/gaol_interval_lexer.lpp: numbers in base 16, 8 and 2
	(0x10, 0xff.8, 0o17, 0b101, 0b1.1p3) are read, the exponent after the
	p being optional; a number that begins with 0 is decimal.

	Writing intervals and the messages of the exceptions:

	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (operator<<): the text is
	made in a string and written at once, in 30 to 45 % less time
	than with a std::ostringstream for each interval and each
	bound; with std::internal the fill follows the sign or the 0x.
	The bounds format is written in the C locale whatever the
	locale of the stream, so that the reader reads it back
	([0,25, 0,5] under a decimal comma before); the width format
	writes the radius without a sign under showpos and a center 0
	as 0; the agreeing format writes the digits two bounds share
	only for finite bounds, not 0, of the same sign and aligned.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (display_bounds): a point
	interval whose double the digits write exactly is written [a],
	the literal of IEEE 1788-2015, and a zero of either sign [0]
	([0x0p+0] in hexadecimal), by operator<< and intervalToText,
	instead of <a, a>; the other points are written [l, r], which
	the reader takes back (interval(0.1) is [0.1,
	0.1000000000000001]); the reader still reads <a, b>.
	* gaol/core/gaol_interval_core.cpp (operator<<): the format
	width writes the midpoint() and rad() of IEEE 1788-2015
	(radius rounded upward, a point interval without radius),
	center the midpoint alone, and the empty set is [empty] in
	every format.
	* gaol/gaol_ieee1788.cpp (intervalToText): [l, r], [a] or
	[empty] with the digits of interval::precision(), whatever the
	output format and the locale, so that textToInterval() reads the
	text back.
	* gaol/gaol_exceptions.h, gaol/gaol_exceptions.cpp:
	gaol_exception::what() returns the explanation, or the name of the
	class (gaol_exception, input_format_error...) if there is none or
	if it starts with a NUL, where GAOL 4 left "std::exception"; a null
	const char* explanation is none, where GAOL 4 built a std::string
	from it; operator<< of the exceptions writes the explanation once.
	* gaol/gaol_exceptions.cpp, gaol/gaol_common.cpp: a null file
	name is "(unknown file)" in gaol_exception (file() is never a
	null pointer), gaol_warning() and gaol_error(), and a null
	message of the two functions is an empty text; libstdc++ put
	std::cerr in the bad state for a null pointer, and the
	diagnostics written after it were lost (gaol_detail::known_file()
	and known_text() of gaol/gaol_common.h).

	Tests, examples and documentation:

	* tests/CMakeLists.txt (gaol_compile_test), tests/compile_error.cmake:
	the compile tests whose compilation has to fail
	(refused_finite_math_only, refused_fast_math, refused_aligned_new,
	refused_aligned_new_cxx17, nodiscard_discard_cxx11,
	nodiscard_discard_cxx14, nodiscard_discard_cxx17) compile a copy of
	their source, which compile_error.cmake makes again and touches before
	the build: they kept failing once their object had been compiled and a
	header, or the source, was restored with its former modification time.
	* tests/input_output.cpp (test_input): <-inf,-inf> and <inf,inf> have
	to throw input_format_error; no try around the checks, which skipped
	the six after <-inf,-inf>.
	* tests/, examples/15_text_and_ieee1788.cpp: the checks of an
	exception, and of a text the reader refuses, are under
	GAOL_EXCEPTIONS_ENABLED, so that the tests and the examples build and
	run without exceptions (--disable-exceptions,
	-Denable-exception=false).  .github/workflows/build-systems.yml: an
	autotools job and a meson job without exceptions.
	* manual/check_examples.py: new; the examples of the manual that show
	their output in their listing (@outputs in gaol.tex) are compiled with
	an installed GAOL and run, and have to print it.
	.github/workflows/linux.yml runs it in the job Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64
	GCC, in C++11.
	* examples/16_Goldstein_Price.cpp, manual/v5/gaol.tex:
	goldstein_price() and the program of the manual have the factor
	(x + y + 1)^2, as the function does (GAOL 4 had (x + y)^2); the
	example checks its output and prints its time in ms; the manual
	says that z encloses the range of the function.
	* manual/v4/manual.cls, manual/build-pdf.sh,
	.github/workflows/manual.yml: the manual of GAOL 4 compiles
	again (marginbib.sty and multicol.sty are gone, bibentry
	prints the references in the margin, Latin Modern fonts); make
	-C manual pdf and the target pdf of meson build it, and a
	workflow builds both manuals.
	* README.md, examples/tutorial.md, doc/using.md, manual/v5: a first
	program, a tutorial of four algorithms of interval computing, the
	common errors (intervals in std::sort, std::set and std::max, and
	gaol::lexicographic_less), the names of IBEX, Codac, C-XSC and
	Boost.Interval next to GAOL's, and the operations that leave the
	rounding direction as they found it.
	* tests/CMakeLists.txt, tests/find_package/CMakeLists.txt,
	tests/fetch_content/CMakeLists.txt: the cache variable
	GAOL_NUMBERS_TIMEOUT (300 s) is the time allowed to the test
	numbers.

